{"n":53,"paragraphs":["Swamiji once shared a story he had heard from a well-known scholar in the West, who had it directly from a Governor of Ceylon. The Governor had watched the whole thing with his own eyes.","A girl was led out and made to sit cross-legged on a little stool built from crossed sticks. She stayed there quietly for a while. Then the performer began pulling the sticks away, one by one. When the last of them was gone, the girl did not fall. She simply remained where she was, sitting in the empty air.","The Governor was convinced he was being fooled. There had to be some hidden support. So he pulled out his sword and swept it hard through the space under the girl. The blade met nothing at all.","Swamiji pointed out that, to Indians, an event like this was not some wonder or conjuring trick. Hardly anyone in India would deny that such things happen; for a Hindu they were simply a fact of life. He added that when Hindus spoke of facing their enemies, they would often say that one of their yogis would come and drive the whole lot away. That was how deeply the people believed in the power of the inner life.","His conclusion was clear. Arms and swords hold no real strength of their own. True power lies in the spirit."],"learn":"We often look for strength in outside things: money, gadgets, status, connections. This story points to a deeper source, the trained and concentrated mind. Building that inner strength through discipline and focus is something no one can take from you.","source":"Vol. 2, p. 21","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}